          { name: "Renter-Household Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 58.8% renter rate, nearly six of ten households in TN-HD-28 have no equity stake in housing, live with direct exposure to housing cost volatility, and face displacement risk that shapes voting behavior and household financial stress. Affordable housing policy, tenant protections, rental market regulation, and housing development that serves households at median income of $45,989 are not optional talking points — they are the material foundation of constituent economic security and the primary driver of housing-insecure voter engagement or demobilization. A representative who can demonstrate concrete legislative wins on tenant protections, affordable housing production, or rent stabilization demonstrates understanding of what actually shapes daily life for the district's majority-renting constituency." },
          { name: "Workforce Development & Economic Mobility for Working-Class Black, White, and Hispanic Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "With median household income of $45,989, college attainment at 24.4%, and a median age of 34.0, TN-HD-28 is a working-class district where younger adults and the 52.44% Black majority face active constraints on economic mobility. The Hispanic community at 12.53% is similarly positioned in the lower-income and lower-credentialed segments of the labor market. Workforce development policy, apprenticeship access, community college funding, and labor-market regulations that protect workers in entry-level and service-sector jobs are the concrete policy tools through which a representative can demonstrate responsiveness to the economic concerns of these communities — moving beyond symbolic acknowledgment to tangible legislation that affects the cost of living and career trajectory." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access for Lower-Income & Majority-Renting Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "In a district with median income of $45,989 and 58.8% renters — many of whom lack employer-sponsored healthcare coverage — healthcare access and cost are direct household concerns that shape voting behavior and constituent satisfaction. The Black community (52.44%) and Hispanic community (12.53%) face documented disparities in healthcare access and preventive care. A representative who can articulate and deliver on healthcare policy — Medicaid expansion advocacy, community health center funding, maternal health access in underserved areas — demonstrates understanding of the material health and financial security concerns that shape this constituency's priorities. However, healthcare is also an area where partisan positioning can substitute for results, and the constituency evaluates based on whether representative action improves actual access and cost, not rhetoric alone." },
          { name: "Treating 75% as Permission for Inattention to Constituent Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% margin in a heavily Democratic district creates the incumbent risk of taking the base for granted and scaling back constituent engagement to levels that do not match the material economic vulnerabilities of a median-income-$45,989, 58.8% renting, 24.4% college-educated constituency. A primary challenger with demonstrated fluency on affordable housing, workforce development, and healthcare access can mobilize frustration with incumbent inattention and credibly argue that the incumbent has stopped delivering on the economic concerns that organized the coalition. The 75% is a floor conditional on consistent responsiveness to constituent priorities, not a stable entitlement to office." },
          { name: "Partisan Positioning as Substitute for Policy Delivery on Material Household Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a safe-seat district, the incumbent temptation is to default to partisan messaging and abstract ideological positioning while scaling back the difficult, unglamorous work of constituent service and legislative delivery on the specific policies that improve household economic security. A constituency that is 58.8% renting, median income $45,989, and 34.0 median age evaluates representatives on whether they have demonstrably improved housing affordability, healthcare access, and job prospects — not on whether they delivered the right partisan talking points. An incumbent who campaigns on broad Democratic messaging without visible legislative wins on the economic concerns this constituency lives with daily will find that primary-election mobilization can materialize around a challenger who promises concrete delivery." }
