          { name: "Taking 71% as Indicative of Stable Constituency Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "The 71% Republican result reflects current affluent homeowner alignment with Republican tax and education policy, but this alignment is contingent on whether state policy actually delivers on those priorities. A suburban constituency at $89,234 income with 36.6% college attainment is also a swing constituency capable of dramatic shifts if they conclude either party better serves their material interests — as demonstrated by suburban migration patterns during recent national cycles. Treating 71% as a durable floor rather than a conditional majority dependent on specific policy delivery is strategically dangerous." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter & Minority Community Concerns as Non-Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "With 27.4% renters and approximately 23.4% non-white population, HD-29 contains roughly 50,000+ constituents whose concerns around housing affordability, wage policy, healthcare access, and educational equity for minority students are real and present. Any representative who treats these populations as peripheral or assumes their votes are not in play because of partisan history is making a structural miscalculation. These constituencies can become organized and decisive if they perceive neglect, particularly as Nashville's metro diversity continues to increase and younger adults comprise a larger share of suburban voters." }
