          { name: "Renter Economic Security and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31% renter rate is the single largest strategic vulnerability in TN-HD-33's apparent Republican solidity. Renters at $61,760 median household income are cost-constrained households whose housing stability is a direct economic concern, not a luxury issue. The Democratic 32% opposition likely overrepresents renters whose economic concerns are not being addressed by the current representative; any candidate who builds an active renter engagement strategy around housing affordability, fair rental practices, and cost-of-living protection can credibly compete for persuadable renters in the middle-income range. Scarbrough must actively demonstrate concern for renter household security to prevent erosion of the working-class coalition." },
          { name: "Local Job Quality and Workforce Development", tag: "careful", why: "The 25.5% college-educated rate and $61,760 median income suggest a constituency employed in local services, trades, light manufacturing, and healthcare rather than high-credentialed sectors. The availability of locally-accessible jobs that pay above the median—and the legislative support for vocational training, apprenticeship, and manufacturing attraction—is a genuine economic development concern for working-age households. This is an area where a Republican incumbent has a natural advantage but must deliver visible results; if the district experiences manufacturing decline or wage stagnation, a credible challenger can argue that state legislative support for workforce development has been inadequate." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization Messaging Over Economic Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "TN-HD-33's 68% Republican result is conditional on the constituency's perception that Republican governance delivers economic competence, not on ideological commitment to partisan conflict. Any candidate who leads with partisan attacks, culture-war messaging, or abstract conservative ideology rather than specific economic accomplishments is misreading the district's actual voting motivation. The 32% opposition in a working-class constituency of this income level reflects real economic concerns, not ideological opposition—and talking past those concerns by emphasizing partisan divisions rather than shared economic interest is electorally counterproductive." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as a Safe Majority That Permits Neglect of Opposition Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68-32 margin should not be confused with permission to ignore the one-third of constituents actively choosing a different candidate. The renter constituency, younger adults, and working-class households experiencing wage stagnation or healthcare cost pressure are real voters in TN-HD-33 who represent the opposition's foundation. Any incumbent who assumes the 68% is a stable floor while declining to engage what motivated the 32% is vulnerable to erosion if economic conditions worsen or if a challenger emerges who speaks credibly to cost-of-living concerns. This district is held by demonstrating that government is delivering for working-class households, not by assuming partisan loyalty." }
