          "The 24% Democratic floor in a district this demographically Republican suggests either strong local opposition to the incumbent specifically, or genuine economic anxiety among working-class and renting households that the current representative is not addressing. At $59,965 median income with 22.3% renter occupancy, this is a constituency experiencing pressure on housing costs, healthcare affordability, and wage stability—issues that do not automatically resolve into partisan loyalty when cost-of-living pressures mount. The 43.2 median age and 20.4% college attainment indicate an older, less-credentialed workforce that is economically vulnerable to labor market shifts and healthcare cost inflation. Any challenger who can articulate concrete answers to working-class economic security—healthcare affordability, housing cost management, wage floors—while the incumbent assumes the 76% is structural rather than earned could rapidly shift the competitive landscape. For the incumbent, the strategic imperative is to understand whether the 24% opposition is localized and addressable, or whether it reflects broader economic grievances that require active governing attention rather than partisan consolidation."
