          { name: "Positioning Non-College-Educated Voters as a Minority Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "With 82.3% of HD-40 lacking a four-year college degree, messaging or framing that positions college-educated people as the economic or cultural reference point, or that treats non-degree workers as a demographic problem rather than the core constituency, will alienate the overwhelming majority of the district. Any rhetoric that valorizes college credentials, urban professionals, or credentialed expertise as superior to working-class knowledge and skill will read as contemptuous to a constituency where the incumbent depends on working-class loyalty; this framing mistake can erode the 84% margin more effectively than any Democratic challenger." },
          { name: "Treating the 84% Supermajority as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "An 84-16 result is a supermajority, but it is built on a constituency under material economic pressure — rising housing costs, healthcare inflation, stagnant wages — whose support for Republican governance is contingent on whether Republican policies are delivering concrete improvements to their lives. In the context of sustained economic deterioration or governance failures that appear to abandon working-class concerns, an 84% margin can erode to 70%, 65%, or lower more rapidly than the current partisan lean would predict. The incumbent should treat this seat as requiring active economic performance and constituent attention, not as a seat where Republican partisanship guarantees the outcome regardless of incumbent behavior or local economic conditions." }
