          { name: "Housing Affordability & Tenant Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 45.9% of households renting and a median income of $68,808, housing cost burden is the most immediate economic pressure on TN-HD-52's constituency. Legislation addressing eviction prevention, rent regulation, tenant rights, and affordable housing development directly improves housing security for nearly half the district's households and is the most concrete way a state representative demonstrates delivery to the coalition that produced the 69% result. This is not a peripheral issue for a young, multiethnic, renter-majority district—it is the primary economic security concern." },
          { name: "Multiethnic Coalition Constituent Service & Community-Specific Advocacy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's three largest demographic groups—white (39.71%), Black (31.46%), and Hispanic (22.96%)—require tailored constituent attention and community-specific legislative advocacy rather than generic diversity rhetoric. Each community faces distinct barriers in employment, healthcare access, education quality, and municipal services, and a representative who builds deep relationships within each community and advocates for their specific needs consolidates the 69% coalition more effectively than one who treats the diverse constituency as a single voting bloc. This is active political work, not demographic inevitability." },
          { name: "Understanding the 31% Opposition & Its Economic Drivers", tag: "careful", why: "A 69-31 margin in a young, diverse, urban district suggests the opposition is not simply ideological conservatism but reflects specific governance frustrations or economic concerns unaddressed by the current representative. The 31% opposition likely includes renters frustrated by housing insecurity, working families concerned about wage stagnation, and parents frustrated by school performance—all concerns that a credible challenger could activate if the incumbent does not directly address them. Identifying whether the 31% is concentrated among specific demographic groups or economic concerns, and offering concrete legislative responses, is essential to preventing margin erosion." },
          { name: "Treating Young, Renter-Majority Demographics as a Stable Base", tag: "avoid", why: "At median age 33.8 with 45.9% renters, TN-HD-52 is a constituency that evaluates representatives on material economic outcomes—housing security, job quality, healthcare access, education—not inherited partisan loyalty. A representative who assumes this demographic composition produces durable Democratic margins without continuous delivery on concrete economic concerns is strategically vulnerable; young renters in working-class neighborhoods are capable of switching allegiance if they perceive the incumbent is not addressing their cost-of-living pressures." },
          { name: "Emphasizing Racial or Ethnic Identity Without Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "While TN-HD-52's multiethnic composition is politically salient, the district's coalition is fundamentally united by economic concerns—housing, healthcare, jobs—not by shared racial or ethnic identity. A representative who leads with racial equity messaging without demonstrating concrete legislative delivery on housing affordability, healthcare access, and wage protection risks appearing performative to constituencies facing material economic pressure. The connection between equity and economic security is real, but it requires explicit articulation and policy demonstration, not assumption." }
