          { name: "Urban Livability and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 31.3-year median age, 49.2% renter rate, and $98K income in Nashville's urban core creates a constituency concerned with housing cost-to-income ratios, zoning reform for density and affordability, and urban neighborhood quality. Despite high income, renters in affluent urban districts face housing affordability pressure driven by demand and limited supply; legislative positions on zoning, development, and housing policy have direct material impact on the district's cost of living. This is both a values issue (progressive urban development) and a practical economics issue for younger professionals building careers in Nashville." },
          { name: "Constituent Service Visibility and Responsiveness", tag: "careful", why: "The 49.2% renter rate and 31.3-year median age signal a younger, more mobile constituency less bound by long-term community ties and more likely to evaluate representation on specific responsiveness and visible effectiveness. An unopposed election can create complacency in constituent service operations; in a district where voters have high education levels and sophistication, perceived unresponsiveness or inaccessibility becomes a vulnerability for future competition. Regular office hours, visible community engagement, and prompt case resolution are not optional in a district with such high expectations for government performance." },
          { name: "Partisan Take for Granted Assumptions", tag: "avoid", why: "Freeman's unopposed 100% victory should not lead to the assumption that 77% college-educated, affluent urban voters are locked in permanently or that representing them requires no ongoing engagement. Younger professionals (31.3 median age) and renters (49.2%) are less tied to place and party; they evaluate on delivery. A complacent incumbent who assumes Democratic dominance and reduces constituent engagement invites a challenger from within the Democratic coalition who can claim greater responsiveness and alignment with progressive priorities." },
          { name: "Abstract Partisan Positioning Over Constituent Problem-Solving", tag: "avoid", why: "TN-HD-56's college-educated, high-income constituency is sophisticated enough to distinguish between performative partisan rhetoric and concrete legislative problem-solving. A representative who defaults to generic Democratic messaging without specific solutions to the district's actual issues—education funding mechanisms, zoning and housing supply, transit and infrastructure, environmental policy implementation—risks being perceived as undifferentiated and as failing to serve the district's specific governance demands. Effective representation in this district requires detailed policy knowledge and constituent-specific legislative advocacy, not party loyalty affirmation." }
