          { name: "Fiscal Stewardship & Economic Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "Affluent, college-educated homeowners at $106,455 median income with 45% college attainment evaluate primarily on fiscal responsibility, tax policy, and tangible economic outcomes—not cultural messaging. The 68% margin is held by delivering on economic stewardship; any stumble on budget management, tax fairness, or government efficiency will activate the 32% opposition and make the seat vulnerable to a competent Democratic challenger who positions on fiscal discipline." },
          { name: "High-Income Professional & Suburban Voter Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "One in three educated, high-income voters in TN-HD-57 already votes Democratic, and another segment is genuinely persuadable toward moderate Democratic candidates perceived as serious and pragmatic. The incumbent's coalition depends on continuous validation that the Republican candidate is the more competent steward of affluent households' economic interests; any opening—incumbent missteps, weak governance record, or tone-deaf cultural positioning—creates space for a credible Democratic challenger to peel off educated voters who would otherwise default Republican." },
          { name: "Property Tax Policy & Cost of Living for High-Income Homeowners", tag: "careful", why: "With 82% homeownership and $106K+ income, property taxes and the cost of living for affluent households are material governance concerns; however, this is not a low-income district struggling with affordability but rather a wealthy constituency evaluating whether their tax burden is managed fairly and efficiently. A legislator who can demonstrate that property tax policy is rational, competitive with neighboring districts, and deployed effectively is addressing a real concern; one who ignores it or frames taxes as simply 'inevitable' will find educated voters migrating toward Democratic candidates who appear to have a more coherent tax philosophy." },
          { name: "Culture-War Positioning That Alienates College-Educated Swing Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "The 32% opposition and 45% college attainment in an affluent district indicate a constituency that has other political homes if it perceives Republican messaging as extreme, anti-institutional, or dismissive of education and expertise. Language around education policy, cultural issues, or institutional reform that reads as ideologically driven rather than pragmatic will activate Democratic support among affluent, educated voters who otherwise might default to the Republican on fiscal grounds—a constituency that appears substantial enough to swing the seat if mobilized against the incumbent." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as a Durable, Structural Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68% result in a district this affluent and educated is not a structural Republican advantage but a coalition-dependent outcome vulnerable to challenger quality and incumbent performance on fiscal stewardship. The 32% opposition in an affluent district is analytically equivalent to 47% opposition in a rural district—it represents real political capacity and voter persuadability among affluent, educated, mobile homeowners who evaluate on competence rather than partisan loyalty. Any incumbent who campaigns as though the seat is secure rather than continuously validating economic stewardship risks losing educated voters to a competent Democratic challenger." }
