          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 69% renter rate with $51,272 median household income in Nashville's urban core, housing cost burden is the primary constraint on economic mobility and household stability. Renters in this income range are likely paying 40-50% of income for housing, leaving minimal resources for healthcare, childcare, and savings. A legislative agenda focused on affordable housing development, renter protections, tenant rights enforcement, and reducing transportation costs through transit investment directly addresses the material economic concerns of the district's constituency majority. This is not abstract policy — it is the difference between whether young families can build economic stability or remain trapped in rent cycles." },
          { name: "Economic Opportunity & Workforce Development for Young Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 31.1-year median age and 36.1% college attainment, HD-58 is a young constituency for whom education-to-employment pathways and wage-level job access are the primary mechanisms for economic mobility. The $51,272 median income reflects a constituency that is working but not prospering, and the gap between entry-level wages and housing costs is the core constraint on advancement. Programs connecting young adults to skilled employment, apprenticeships, and sectors with genuine career mobility — coupled with childcare affordability and transit access that reduces transportation barriers — are the tangible investments that determine whether this constituency sees government as an active partner in their economic mobility or as irrelevant to their constraints." },
          { name: "Community Trust & Constituent Service Visibility", tag: "careful", why: "The unopposed 2024 victory may indicate authentic constituent satisfaction with the incumbent, or it may indicate the absence of a credible challenger — not necessarily durable political alignment in a young, economically stressed district. In communities with constrained resources and institutional distrust, representative visibility, accessibility, and demonstrated problem-solving on individual constituent issues are the primary mechanisms through which residents evaluate whether they can trust government. An incumbent who maintains regular district presence, responsive constituent service, and visible problem-solving on housing and employment barriers builds political durability; one who relies on the unopposed status as a substitute for active constituent engagement creates vulnerability that an organized challenger can exploit." },
          { name: "Treating Black Constituents as a Monolithic Political Block", tag: "avoid", why: "While 56.3% of HD-58 is Black, treating this constituency as a unified voting bloc without variation in priorities, economic status, and political engagement misses the actual diversity within the Black community — which includes renters and homeowners, young families and older adults, employed and underemployed residents with different material concerns. Specific engagement on issues affecting Black renters (housing affordability, eviction protection), Black workers (wage levels, workforce development), and Black families (childcare, education) is more effective than abstract appeals to racial identity or inherited Democratic loyalty. This is particularly important given the 69% renter rate, which means the economic stakes are different for Black constituents than in districts with higher homeownership." },
          { name: "Using Unopposed Status as a Substitute for Legislative Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 victory is not a mandate to pursue an agenda disconnected from constituent material concerns — it is a temporary absence of formal opposition that can evaporate if the incumbent's record diverges from district expectations. In a young, economically stressed, majority-renter constituency with 56% Black population, legislative inaction on housing affordability, renter protections, or economic opportunity creates latent vulnerability. Any incumbent who uses unopposed status as political cover to avoid difficult tradeoffs between business interests and renter protections, or to neglect responsive constituent service, risks facing a credible challenger in the next cycle who offers a more genuine commitment to district economic security." }
