          { name: "Younger Adult Workforce Development and Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 38.1 median age and 28.3% college-attainment rate signal a working-age district where post-secondary education is a legitimate question but not universal, and where trade, technical, and workforce-development pathways are materially important. Younger adults in HD-64 are evaluating whether economic opportunity exists in the district or whether migration is necessary — a calculation that directly determines the district's long-term demographic and economic trajectory. A legislative agenda that treats workforce development, apprenticeship programs, and local job creation as core priorities rather than secondary concerns resonates authentically with this age cohort." },
          { name: "Native American Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~4% demographic gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% likely represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents whose presence in the district is real and whose governance expectations deserve authentic attention. While this gap is smaller than in some comparable districts, it represents approximately 2,500-3,000 residents whose civic participation and policy concerns on tribal sovereignty, healthcare access, and economic development are legitimate legislative matters. Any representative who acknowledges this constituency only in performative terms will miss the opportunity to build genuine relationships with a meaningful voting bloc." },
          { name: "Treating Renter Concerns as Marginal or Secondary", tag: "avoid", why: "A representative who frames primary economic concerns through homeowner or property-owner interests while treating renter and working-household concerns as side issues is governing a district that is smaller than the one that actually exists. At 32% renter rate, renters are not a niche interest — they are a substantial constituency whose economic security directly depends on whether government is addressing their material concerns. Candidates who dismiss housing affordability or cost-of-living as non-essential create an opening for challengers to build credibility on these issues among a constituency that has nowhere else to look for representation." },
          { name: "Assuming 70% Represents a Durable Floor Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70-30 result in a $72,983-income district with 32% renters and a 38.1 median age is not a mandate for hands-off governance — it is a strong but conditional majority that depends on whether the incumbent is actively serving the economic concerns of younger adults and working households. The 30% opposition in this income bracket represents substantial numbers of people evaluating whether the incumbent is delivering on material concerns, and a representative who campaigns this seat as though 70% is a guaranteed floor, rather than a margin that must be actively maintained through constituent service, is vulnerable to compression if a credible alternative emerges on cost-of-living or economic opportunity." }
