          { name: "Workforce Development & Vocational Education", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 75.7% of adults lacking four-year degrees, this is a college-underrepresented district where community college funding, trade school accessibility, and apprenticeship programs are direct economic-mobility concerns. A Democratic agenda that treats vocational education and skills training as co-equal to four-year higher education, with specific state funding commitments, speaks directly to the material concerns of a constituency that has historically heard little from Republican messaging on this specific ladder of opportunity." },
          { name: "Healthcare Cost Management for Moderate-Income Families", tag: "careful", why: "At $77,867 median income, the district's households are precisely the income level most vulnerable to healthcare cost shocks and insurance gaps — but this is also an area where Republican and Democratic messaging converge on cost concern while diverging sharply on solutions. Any candidate claiming to address healthcare affordability must ground claims in specific legislative mechanisms (insurance regulation, prescription drug policy, coverage expansion) rather than abstract commitment, or will lose credibility with a constituency skeptical of partisan healthcare rhetoric." },
          { name: "Tax Policy Framed as Wealthy-vs.-Working-Class", tag: "avoid", why: "While tax fairness is a material concern for moderate-income households, leading with abstract anti-wealthy rhetoric risks alienating the small-business owners and upper-middle-class homeowners who form the constituency's stable core. Frame tax policy around whether state governance delivers tangible benefits to working families — schools, roads, healthcare access — rather than redistributive appeals that feel alien to a homeowner-majority district with modest but meaningful asset holdings." },
          { name: "Treating the 74% Margin as Indicative of Stable Republican Dominance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result in a district with moderate income ($77,867), low college attainment (24.3%), and significant renter population (18.7%) is not a demographic mandate — it is a current outcome reflecting the incumbent's positioning and constituent satisfaction with delivery on material concerns. Any Republican candidate who campaigns on the assumption that 74% represents a structural floor rather than an outcome vulnerable to constituent frustration with unaddressed economic concerns will be surprised by a credible Democratic opponent who runs a disciplined campaign grounded in this district's actual economic interests." }
