          { name: "Working-Class & Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23% renter rate and $70,492 median household income together identify a constituency of working-class households with modest economic cushion and significant vulnerability to cost shocks in housing, healthcare, and utilities. Renters in this income bracket are particularly sensitive to housing affordability, wage stagnation, and whether government is delivering concrete economic benefit or simply managing decline in their material circumstances. The unopposed incumbent has not demonstrated specific policy commitments or messaging directed at this group's concerns, creating an opening for a challenger to build persuasion through targeted economic messaging and concrete policy proposals on housing costs, wage growth, and cost-of-living relief." },
          { name: "AIAN Community & the 4% Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The approximately 4% gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents roughly 1,000-1,200 residents who are likely AIAN and deserve explicit engagement and representation as a constituency. While this population is smaller than the Black community, it is concentrated enough that mobilization around specific issues — tribal healthcare access, cultural institution funding, or economic partnership — could shift outcomes in a close election. An incumbent who has not yet built relationships with AIAN residents is vulnerable to a challenger who does, and any representative should have clear commitments on Indian Health Service access and tribal economic development as basic constituent service expectations." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Win as Evidence of Electoral Durability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election provides zero information about whether the incumbent has actually consolidated the full constituency, whether there are areas of latent dissatisfaction, or whether specific communities feel engaged and represented by their legislator. The absence of a challenger is a structural advantage, not a proof of durable support — and any incumbent who campaigns the next election cycle as though an unopposed win demonstrates firm control of the district is strategically misreading the data. The risk is complacency in constituent engagement, particularly among Black voters and renters who have not yet seen a contested election or experienced a challenger offering alternative leadership." },
          { name: "Dismissing the Hispanic (3.12%) and AIAN (4%) Communities as Electorally Insignificant", tag: "avoid", why: "While both communities are small in percentage terms, they represent real residents with concrete needs around economic opportunity, healthcare access, and education quality. An incumbent who focuses exclusively on the white homeowner majority and treats these communities as demographically negligible is both failing to serve constituents and creating an opening for a challenger to build surprising persuasion leverage among voters who have never experienced direct engagement or policy attention from their representative. In a district without demonstrated electoral competition, small communities can become the difference between a comfortable win and a contested race if mobilized effectively." }
