          { name: "Black Community Civic Infrastructure and Institutional Partnership", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 67.64% of the district, the Black population is the electoral and civic foundation of TN-HD-86, and the 78% Democratic result reflects deep institutional trust rooted in civil rights history, ongoing community organization, and proven responsiveness to constituent needs. This trust is built and maintained through authentic engagement with the institutions that anchor Black community civic life—churches, community development organizations, neighborhood associations, schools, and grassroots organizations—not through campaign appearances. A representative who has genuine roots in these institutions, who shows up consistently with specific policy knowledge, and who can demonstrate accountability for governance outcomes commands the credibility and constituent relationships that justify and sustain the 78% margin. Any candidate who treats the 78% as a given rather than as something earned through sustained community partnership will discover that those relationships are fragile." },
          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Development for Younger Adults", tag: "careful", why: "The 34.7-year median age is substantially younger than state median, and the 26.7% college attainment reflects both economic barriers to higher education and skill gaps that constrain upward mobility. Younger adults in this district are evaluating representation on whether government is creating genuine pathways to economic opportunity—not merely managing poverty or delivering services. Vocational training, apprenticeship programs, job placement outcomes, and the connection between workforce development and actual employment are the metrics by which younger constituents assess whether their representative is investing in their economic future. The 78% Democratic margin should not be mistaken as permission to deprioritize these outcomes; younger voters with constrained economic opportunity are susceptible to candidates who can credibly demonstrate commitment to improving their material position." },
          { name: "Treating Supermajority as Permission to Disengage from Constituent Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 78% result is not evidence that the seat is secure or that constituent engagement can be deprioritized; it reflects deep community trust earned through proven responsiveness to material concerns and sustained institutional partnership. Supermajorities erode quickly when constituents perceive that elected officials have stopped listening, have become disconnected from community institutions, or are no longer delivering on economic priorities. In a district where 54.3% of households rent and median income is $50,627, constituents are evaluating representation on concrete results—housing policy, economic mobility, healthcare access—not rhetoric. Any candidate who treats the 78% as permission to reduce constituent engagement, community listening, or accountability will find that trust diminishes rapidly." },
          { name: "Positioning as an Outsider Challenger to Democratic Institutional Leadership", tag: "avoid", why: "The 78% Democratic margin and the deep institutional trust reflected in that result create significant electoral risk for any candidate attempting to run as an insurgent against Democratic institutional leadership or established community organizations. The Black community institutions that form the foundation of this district's Democratic coalition—churches, community organizations, civic leaders—are trusted governance partners with whom constituents have ongoing relationships. A challenger or new candidate who attempts to gain traction by positioning established institutional leadership as corrupt, ineffective, or unrepresentative is arguing against the actual lived experience of constituents who rely on these institutions daily. This positioning is particularly high-risk in a majority-Black district where institutional trust and community leadership are core sources of political power and civic resilience." }
