        issues: [
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 57.2% renters — more than half the district — housing cost burden is the direct economic issue affecting a majority of households. At $50,966 median income, renters in HD-90 are spending 35-40% of household income on rent, creating genuine economic stress and vulnerability to displacement. Tenant protections, rental assistance, eviction prevention, and affordable housing development are the material policy levers that affect whether working families can stay housed. An incumbent who delivers on these issues becomes genuinely indispensable; an incumbent who neglects them creates a credible opening for opposition." },
          { name: "Workforce Development & Economic Mobility for Non-College-Educated Workers", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 33.6% college attainment in a district of 35.8 median age, the majority of working-age adults lack four-year degrees and are competing in a labor market increasingly stratified by credentials. Apprenticeships, technical training, community college access, and employer partnerships that create stable pathways to $60,000+ incomes are the concrete policy response to what is otherwise a constituency caught between wage stagnation and credential inflation. A representative who builds relationships with regional employers and community colleges and delivers measurable skill-training placements becomes materially valuable; one who defaults to abstract messaging becomes vulnerable." },
          { name: "Diverse Working-Class Community Engagement Across Racial & Ethnic Lines", tag: "careful", why: "With 72.64% white, 11.39% Black, and 9.26% Hispanic residents, HD-90 lacks a single demographic supermajority, meaning that no constituency can be taken for granted and all require specific constituent attention. The Hispanic and Black communities' economic concerns — workforce discrimination, healthcare access, small business lending — can become invisible in a district where the 72% white majority drives aggregate messaging. If Black and Hispanic constituents perceive the incumbent as delivering primarily for white working-class interests, coalition coherence fractures and opposition gains inroads." },
          { name: "Treating 100% as Evidence of Durable Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "An uncontested 100% result is not evidence of Democratic strength or constituent satisfaction; it is evidence that Republican resources were allocated elsewhere. The moment opposition emerges with a specific economic message about housing costs, healthcare access, or wage growth targeted at a $50,966-income, 57.2%-renter, 35.8-year-median-age working-class constituency, the absence of incumbent relationship-building and baseline constituent engagement becomes a serious liability. Assuming 100% reflects durable support rather than non-competition is a strategic mistake that leaves the seat vulnerable." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Messaging Over Material Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-class, rent-burdened, non-college-educated constituency at median income $50,966, abstract partisan positioning — even Democratic positioning — is less electorally powerful than demonstration of material benefit. Renters care about eviction prevention and rental assistance; non-degree-holding workers care about job training that pays; younger adults care about healthcare access and wage growth. An incumbent who substitutes party loyalty messaging for concrete constituent service on these material issues will find that loyalty is contingent and opposition can make inroads by demonstrating actual alternative plans." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "TN-HD-90: A 100% Democratic Seat with Hidden Vulnerability — Unopposed Victory Masks Deep Economic Stress, High Rentership, and Thin College Attainment That Demand Active Constituent Engagement to Hold.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "TN-HD-90 is an unusual strategic case: Johnson won with 100% in 2024, suggesting an uncontested seat with no Republican challenger, yet the district's demographic profile reveals substantial economic vulnerability and potential openings for future opposition. At $50,966 median household income, 57.2% renter rate, and 33.6% college attainment, HD-90 is a working-class, rent-burdened constituency with limited educational credentials relative to state averages — the exact profile where economic anxiety, housing insecurity, and healthcare access concerns drive voter behavior when opposition emerges. The 100% result is not evidence of durable Democratic strength; it is evidence of Republican resource allocation choices. The moment an organized challenger invests in this seat and articulates a specific economic message to the renter majority and working-class households at $50,966 income, the absence of a Republican baseline and incumbent relationship-building becomes a serious liability.",
          "The demographic composition — 72.64% white, 11.39% Black, 9.26% Hispanic, 2.06% Asian, and ~4.65% gap likely Native American or multiracial — reflects a diverse working-class community where no single group holds overwhelming majority. This diversity means that a representative cannot consolidate the seat through a single constituency base; instead, sustained electoral margins require genuine engagement across multiple communities simultaneously. At 35.8 median age, the district skews younger than state average, meaning that younger renters and early-career workers without college degrees make up a substantial portion of the constituency — a demographic cohort consistently responsive to messaging about housing affordability, wage floors, and student debt. An incumbent who has won unopposed can easily mistake 100% for a durable position; the strategic reality is that the combination of high rentership, modest education levels, working-class income, and relative youth creates a constituency whose support is contingent on active delivery of material benefit and authentic constituent listening, not inherited partisan loyalty."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat 100% as an artifact of non-competition, not evidence of seat strength — the moment a credible challenger invests, the absence of incumbent relationship-building and a working-class constituency at $50,966 income becomes the primary vulnerability; build authentic relationships now with renters, small business owners, and working families before opposition materializes.",
          "Lead with housing affordability and renter protection — at 57.2% renters, more than half the district has direct economic stakes in tenant rights, eviction prevention, rental assistance, and housing cost control; this is the single highest-leverage issue for constituent retention and the most obvious area where a Democratic incumbent can outpace Republican opposition.",
          "Engage the diverse working-class base across racial and ethnic lines — with 72.64% white, 11.39% Black, and 9.26% Hispanic residents, no single community is a majority; specific attention to workforce development, small business support, and healthcare access for each community simultaneously prevents any group from feeling invisible or neglected.",
          "Address the 33.6% college-attainment gap with targeted workforce and skills programming — younger residents at 35.8 median age without college degrees need clear pathways to economic mobility through apprenticeships, technical training, and employer partnerships; a representative who delivers on this becomes materially indispensable rather than dependable."
        ]
