          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Professionals as a Swing Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 52-48 Republican margin in a state that votes 60+ percent Republican reveals that college-educated professionals are genuinely persuadable and are evaluating on material governance outcomes rather than partisan identity. The 55.6% college-educated rate and $89,188 income are strongly predictive of swing-voter behavior nationally; in Tennessee's context, a 4-point margin in this demographic is fragile. Any candidate who assumes this constituency is locked in Republican without active engagement on education, economic mobility, and service delivery is ignoring the actual electoral data from this district and from national patterns among college-educated suburban professionals." },
          { name: "Partisan Cultural Messaging as a Primary Campaign Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency that is 55.6% college-educated, suburban-professional, and economically integrated across racial and ethnic lines does not respond primarily to cultural-conservative messaging; it responds to education quality, infrastructure investment, housing affordability, and whether government demonstrates competent stewardship. Any campaign that leads with cultural-partisan positioning rather than concrete governance proposals on the material concerns of young professionals is misaligned with this district's actual constituency and is likely to underperform with the college-educated cohort that determines the 52-48 margin." },
          { name: "Treating the 48% Opposition as Marginal or Unpersuadable", tag: "avoid", why: "In a state where Republicans win statewide by 15-20 points, a 48% Democratic share represents a genuine, economically-rooted constituency of college-educated professionals who are making deliberate voting choices based on education and economic policy — not default tribal loyalty. The opposition in HD-97 is not fringe; it is college-educated suburban professionals who are reachable with credible governance platforms. Any candidate who consolidates only the Republican base and neglects the 48% as unpersuadable will find this seat genuinely at risk if a credible challenger emerges with education and economic-mobility positions that resonate with the professional-class majority." }
