          { name: "Education Quality & Workforce Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 24.1% college attainment and a median age of 34.2, HD-98 has a younger, working-age constituency where K-12 education quality and post-secondary workforce pathways directly determine economic trajectory. Public school funding, career and technical education access, and partnerships between schools and employers are not abstract policy questions—they are the mechanisms through which a 62.77% Black, working-class community either achieves economic mobility or remains locked in low-wage patterns. A representative who champions school funding adequacy, vocational training, and employer partnerships that create clear pathways from education to quality employment speaks directly to the constraint that most affects this district's younger adults and families." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Medicaid Stability", tag: "careful", why: "With a 62.77% Black population, 47.4% renters, and $57,582 median income, HD-98 has a constituency where healthcare access—particularly preventive care, maternal health, and chronic disease management—is both a health justice and economic security issue. Healthcare costs are a leading driver of household financial instability for working families at this income level, and Medicaid expansion or contraction has direct material impact on constituent wellbeing. This is not a marginal issue; it is a concrete concern for the majority of the district. However, healthcare debates can become abstract quickly, and a representative must connect policy to specific outcomes: how does this position affect whether a constituent's child gets preventive care, or whether a family stays solvent when serious illness strikes." },
          { name: "Over-interpreting the 74% Margin as Stable Electoral Ground", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% Democratic result is substantial, but it does not eliminate the obligation to understand and respond to the 26% opposition or the material constraints that affect the 74% base. The combination of below-median income, high renter concentration, and lower college attainment means this constituency evaluates on tangible economic outcomes, not partisan inheritance. A representative who assumes the 74% is durable because the district is demographically Democratic—rather than because constituents see responsive governance and material improvement in their lives—is vulnerable to primary challenge or erosion if economic conditions worsen or constituent needs go unaddressed." },
          { name: "Treating the Hispanic Community as Peripheral", tag: "avoid", why: "At 10.24% of the district, the Hispanic community is smaller than the Black majority but substantial enough to deserve direct constituent attention and representation. Hispanic residents in a district with 47.4% renters and working-class income face specific concerns around workplace rights, immigration enforcement impact on families and workers, and access to language-appropriate services—issues that can become invisible when the dominant political narrative focuses only on the Black community's needs. Consolidating the existing coalition without active engagement of the Hispanic constituency means leaving political capacity unused and constituent needs unmet, particularly in a district where economic vulnerability affects multiple communities simultaneously." }
