          { name: "Middle-Income Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $56,294 median household income, 42.3-year median age, and 68.3% homeownership define a constituency of established families and pre-retirees for whom fixed costs—property taxes, insurance, utilities, healthcare—are rising faster than incomes. At this income level, a 5-10% increase in property tax or insurance compresses discretionary spending meaningfully. Property tax reform, utility rate regulation, and healthcare cost management are the concrete governance issues most likely to erode or consolidate the 75% margin, making economic security the single most important issue for holding this seat." },
          { name: "Renter Population & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31.7% renter rate is a significant constituency whose voting behavior is more volatile than homeowners and whose economic concerns—rent burden, wage stagnation, housing supply—are distinct from property owners. In a district at 75% Republican, the renter population represents the constituency most open to an alternative candidate, particularly if wages are stagnant while housing costs rise. Specific engagement on entry-level wage policy, housing development incentives, and rental market regulation converts renters from passive opposition into durable support and increases seat durability." },
          { name: "Understanding the 3.6% Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The reported Census categories total 96.4%, leaving 3.6% unaccounted for—which in Tennessee likely represents American Indian/Alaska Native residents, multiracial populations, or other groups whose constituent needs may be invisible in district-level analysis. While the gap is small, it represents real people whose engagement matters both for governance completeness and for the trust-building that makes a 75% margin durable. Any candidate should understand who comprises this gap and engage authentically rather than assuming they are negligible." },
          { name: "Treating 75% as an Ideological Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% Republican result is impressive numerically but reflects conditional satisfaction with economic outcomes among homeowners whose stakes are property values and steady employment—not ideological conviction or deep partisan identification. Treating this margin as a mandate for ideological positioning unrelated to the constituency's material economic concerns risks mistaking the incumbent's success for voter preference about ideology rather than economics. A challenger offering better cost-of-living remedies could quickly narrow this margin regardless of party brand." },
          { name: "Assuming Renter Constituency Passivity", tag: "avoid", why: "At 31.7% of the district, renters represent nearly one-third of the population and are significantly more likely to vote Democratic or for a challenger offering economic remedies than homeowners. An incumbent who consolidates the homeowner base without actively engaging renters on housing affordability and wage issues leaves a large constituency available for mobilization by an opponent. In a district this large, the renter vote is not negligible background noise—it is a viable winning coalition if motivated around specific issues." }
