          { name: "Healthcare Affordability for an Aging, Fixed-Income Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.1 median age and $59,021 household income, TN-SD-8 has significant populations of both aging fixed-income households and younger households under healthcare cost pressure. Rising premiums, deductibles, rural hospital viability, and healthcare access are not abstract policy concerns but direct household budget pressures. Legislative work on prescription drug costs, rural healthcare infrastructure, and insurance affordability—delivered with specificity rather than partisan rhetoric—addresses the material concerns most likely driving the 16% opposition and most capable of threatening the supermajority if left unaddressed." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Economic Security", tag: "careful", why: "At 26.8% renter rate with $59,021 median household income, the district has a substantial population of renters under housing cost pressure—both younger adults entering the market and older adults on fixed incomes. Housing affordability, rental market stability, and pathways to homeownership are material concerns for this constituency. However, housing policy in a 89.92% white, homeowner-majority district can activate defensiveness around property values and tax implications if not framed carefully; the strategic approach is to lead with renter constituency needs while acknowledging homeowner property interests, not as competing priorities but as shared interest in stable housing markets." },
          { name: "Treating 84% as a Stable Supermajority Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "An 84-16 result in a district with working-class economic pressures and non-college demographics reflects current incumbent dominance, not an immovable partisan alignment. At $59,021 income and 43.1 median age, the constituency is materially vulnerable to economic disruption and healthcare cost shock—the kinds of life events that shift electoral behavior. Any representative who treats 84% as permission to ignore the 16% or as a signal that economic constituent concerns have been fully addressed is strategically vulnerable to a challenger who articulates an alternative vision for working-class economic security." },
          { name: "Positioning Non-College Work as Inferior to College Pathways", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 80.8% of adults do not hold a college degree and economic mobility depends on strong non-college pathways, any positioning that implicitly frames college as the default successful outcome or that treats non-college work as a failure pathway will alienate the majority of the constituency. The strategic framing is that multiple pathways to economic security exist and that the state should invest equally in all of them; positioning that valorizes college at the expense of skilled trades and non-college work triggers the kind of cultural resentment that activates opposition votes." }
