          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Engagement & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 15.33% Black and 9.25% Hispanic, these communities represent nearly one-quarter of TN-SD-10's population but are not currently being mobilized to the polls at the intensity that would fully shift the partisan result. Economic opportunity, healthcare access, workforce development, and small-business support are the material concerns for these communities, and a candidate who builds sustained constituent relationships around these issues — rather than token engagement — can shift Democratic performance in a district where the current 39% opposition may include significant demobilization. The 61-39 margin suggests neither party is driving these communities to the polls with sufficient intensity; any candidate who changes this math gains significant leverage." },
          { name: "Understanding the Renter Majority & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 38.9% renters — higher than the national average — TN-SD-10 has a significant constituency for whom housing affordability, tenant protections, and rental market stability are direct economic concerns. This population skews younger and includes working families, service-sector workers, and lower-income households who experience housing cost burden acutely. A legislator who acknowledges renter concerns and develops policy responses (rent stabilization, landlord-tenant protections, affordable housing development) gains credibility with this constituency; one who dismisses renter concerns as irrelevant to 'real property owners' risks activating this swing population around economic messaging that favors the opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Durable Majority Rather Than a Contingent Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 result in a working-class district with $62,014 median income, 38.9% renters, and 29.8% college attainment is not a landslide — it is a majority built on current alignment between the incumbent and the homeowner-plurality base, contingent on economic conditions and constituent perception of whether state policy is delivering. In a presidential election year or against a challenger who nationalizes economic messaging around wage stagnation and housing costs, this margin contracts sharply; any incumbent who campaigns as though 61% represents stable coalition strength rather than a result dependent on economic delivery and limited opposition engagement is strategically vulnerable." },
          { name: "Positioning Renter Concerns or Minority Community Priorities as Special Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "Housing affordability, healthcare access, and workforce opportunity are not special-interest concerns in a district where 38.9% are renters and 24.58% are Black or Hispanic — they are foundational economic issues for nearly one-quarter or more of the constituency. Any candidate whose rhetoric positions these concerns as competing with 'regular constituents' or as secondary to homeowner priorities is explicitly framing away material economic concerns for a significant portion of the district. This framing is not just rhetorically tone-deaf; it actively activates opposition among communities whose support is necessary to win in a close election and whose demobilization in a working-class district represents the greatest vulnerability to economic-messaging-based challenge." }
