          { name: "Workforce Development & Wage-Growth Pathways", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.9% college-attainment rate and 34.6-year median age identify a working-age constituency constrained by educational access and wage mobility. Community college funding, apprenticeship programs, employer partnerships, and training infrastructure are concrete governance levers that directly affect whether a young working-age household can move into higher-wage employment. Candidates who lead with these material economic pathways rather than abstract conservative messaging better reflect the actual priorities of the district's demographically youngest and economically most vulnerable cohort." },
          { name: "Understanding What Sustains the 68% Margin", tag: "careful", why: "A 68-32 result in a district where 37.5% are renters, 25.9% college-educated, and the median age is 34.6 suggests either exceptional incumbent constituent service or meaningful Democratic failure to articulate economic alternatives. The margin is substantially larger than the underlying demographic profile would predict, which means the coalition holding this seat is either personally loyal to the incumbent or ideologically committed to the Republican brand—neither of which is guaranteed to transfer to a successor. Any incumbent or challenger must understand specifically whether the 68% reflects durable political alignment or personal incumbency advantage that does not automatically transfer." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as an Unquestionable Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 37.5% renters facing housing cost pressure, 34.6-year median age, and 25.9% college attainment, the assumption that a 68-32 result is durable ignores the material economic vulnerabilities of the largest working-age cohorts. If a credible challenger emerges who leads with housing affordability, wage growth, and healthcare access—rather than abstract partisan positioning—the margin is substantially more vulnerable than the headline number suggests." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Majority's Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 37.5% renter rate, this constituency is more mobile, more cost-of-living sensitive, and more likely to evaluate candidates on concrete economic delivery than on partisan inheritance. Campaigning TN-SD-14 as if the homeowner base is the decisive constituency while renters are a secondary interest actively invites a challenger who speaks directly to housing affordability and living-cost security to consolidate that 37.5% into a competitive alternative." }
