          { name: "Renter Constituency Economic Vulnerability & Mobilization Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The 26.6% renter rate represents one-quarter of the district's constituency — economically precarious households with different material concerns (housing affordability, wage stability) than homeowners and potentially responsive to Democratic messaging about economic security. In a district currently at 76-24, a shift of 5-8 percentage points among renters toward Democratic candidates, combined with effective turnout mobilization, could move the seat from safe to competitive. Any Republican campaign must address renter economic concerns proactively rather than assuming homeowner loyalty translates to overall district stability." },
          { name: "Treating the 76% Result as a Durable Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76-24 result in a district with constrained median income ($57,823), one-quarter renters, and low college attainment is not a landslide cushion — it is a working-class Republican advantage that remains durable only as long as the constituency sees state government as addressing their material concerns. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 76% is a stable floor without actively engaging the material economic concerns of working-class households will find competitive space opening up if an opponent offers more credible solutions to property tax burden, healthcare costs, or wage stagnation." },
          { name: "Ignoring the ~3.2% Population Gap & Potential American Indian Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 96.78% of the population, leaving a ~3.2% gap that likely represents American Indian residents given Tennessee's geography and tribal presence in the region. In a district at 76-24, even modest mobilization of a previously disengaged or overlooked constituency could shift margins. Any candidate who campaigns without acknowledging or engaging this population segment is leaving potential voters uncontested and failing to represent a part of the actual constituency they claim to serve." }
