          { name: "Minority Community Constituent Engagement & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 19.98% Black, 10.98% Hispanic, and 2.45% Asian populations represent 33.4% of the district's identified base, yet the 61-39 partisan result suggests these communities are not being actively organized or mobilized by the incumbent. In a district where younger adults and renters are majority or near-majority in these communities, specific engagement on education investment, healthcare access, small business support, and economic mobility is both a governance obligation and a strategic opportunity. A challenger who invests authentically in minority constituent relationships can materially improve the opposition's performance without flipping the seat outright." },
          { name: "Educational Attainment & Workforce Development", tag: "careful", why: "College attainment of 31.3%—below the national average of roughly 37%—combined with a median age of 31.7 signals a relatively young constituency with real stakes in K-12 quality, workforce training, and the pathway from secondary education to economic opportunity. In a competitive state and regional environment, education investment and workforce development are both material governance issues and potential points of contestation between incumbents and challengers. This is not a college-town constituency to be taken for granted, nor is it a population indifferent to educational investment." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Durable, Settled Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 22-point margin in a district with a median age of 31.7, a 37% renter rate, and 33.4% minority population is not the same as a 22-point margin in a stable, older, homeowner-dominant district. The incumbent's margin is held by active alignment with the district's working-class and middle-class majority; any representative who campaigns as though 61% is a permanent floor rather than a working result will find that margin compressed when challenged by an opponent who invests in younger voters, renters, and minority constituents ignored by an incumbent taking the seat for granted." },
          { name: "Assuming Minority Communities Are Locked Into the Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 61-39 result shows the Republican hold, the absence of active Republican engagement with the 33.4% minority population suggests an assumption that these voters are inherently Democratic or unwinnable—a dangerous miscalculation in a district where economic concerns transcend partisan identity. A minority community facing housing affordability, healthcare access, or education concerns is open to a representative who delivers concrete results, regardless of party label. Assuming these communities are politically settled is both analytically wrong and strategically wasteful." }
