          { name: "Black Institutional Leadership and Community Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 59% Black majority provides a durable electoral foundation, but that foundation depends on demonstrable investment in and partnership with Nashville's Black institutional anchors — churches, fraternal organizations, civic groups, labor unions, and community development corporations. The 80% Democratic margin reflects both historical alignment and expectation that Democratic representation will prioritize Black community economic development, education investment, and institutional partnership. A representative who maintains deep relationships with these institutions and uses legislative leverage to direct resources toward community-identified priorities strengthens the coalition; one who treats the 59% as a given while focusing energy elsewhere risks primary challenge from a competitor with stronger institutional legitimacy." },
          { name: "Younger Adult Economic Opportunity in a High-Cost Urban Market", tag: "careful", why: "The 34.1-year median age combined with 28.3% college attainment and 55.2% renter rate indicates a significant population of young adults without four-year degrees competing in Nashville's increasingly expensive housing market and service-sector labor market. This constituency is Democratic by electoral alignment but economically vulnerable to private-sector wage suppression and housing cost inflation; if they conclude that Democratic representation is not effectively addressing these constraints, primary challenge or reduced turnout becomes a real risk. Workforce development, vocational training, and housing policy that specifically target this demographic group's economic mobility are not optional legislative priorities in a district this young." },
          { name: "Treating the 80% Supermajority as Permission to Shift Away from Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "Large electoral margins in working-class districts frequently create complacency among incumbents, who interpret the result as a durable base requiring only routine maintenance. In a district where 55.2% are renters, median income is $51,268, and economic margin is tight for the majority of constituents, the supermajority is contingent on demonstrable delivery of improved material conditions — not inherited. An incumbent who treats the 80% as insurance against accountability and reduces investment in constituent service, community listening, and targeted economic development will find that margin erodes, particularly to primary challenge from a competitor with greater perceived commitment to economic improvement." },
          { name: "Pursuing Abstract Partisan Agenda at the Expense of Constituent Economic Priorities", tag: "avoid", why: "The Democratic alignment in TN-SD-30 is rooted in material dependence on public investment in services, housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity — not in ideological commitment to abstract partisan positions. A representative who uses legislative energy on issues disconnected from constituent economic concerns while housing costs, healthcare access, and wage growth remain pressing problems will find the coalition fracture, particularly when a primary challenger can argue more credibly that they will prioritize tangible economic delivery. In this district, partisan symbolism is only valuable if it simultaneously advances the material economic interests of renters, working-age adults, and economically constrained households." }
